- How to use `az` command map:
  - Issue lifecycle:
  - `az issue list --limit 20`, `az issue list --status in_progress --limit 20`, `az issue get <issue-id>`, `az issue create "Child task" [--json]` (immediate child work; auto-parents under `AZEDARACH_ISSUE_ID`){{ if .TmuxAvailable }}, `az issue split "Child task" [--json]` (create isolated child session){{ end }}, `az issue create --deferred "Later backlog task" [--json]` (standalone backlog only; skips auto-parenting and is not a worktree/session control), `az issue update <issue-id> --status in_review|in_progress|open [--json]`, `az issue close <issue-id> [--json]` (closes only when already clean), `az issue close <issue-id> --cleanup [--json]` (run required session/worktree cleanup before close)
  - `az issue update`: use `--update-impl` only when changing implementation assignments; status/title/notes updates do not need it.
  - Status semantics: use `open` for not-yet-active work, `in_progress` while actively working, `in_review` when implementation is complete and waiting for human/orchestrator review or integration, and `closed` only after acceptance/integration is complete.
  - Blocked is not an issue status. Represent blocked work with dependency edges (`az issue dep add <blocked-issue> <blocker-issue> --type blocks`) and record blocker evidence in issue notes or mailbox events.
  - `az issue bulk-create --input issues.json --json`, `az issue bulk-update --input updates.json --json`, `az issue update <issue-id> --design "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --notes "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --append-notes "..."`
